{"type": "answer", "run_id": "chaos-20261110T183141Z", "scenario": "chaos", "source": "stand-in", "setting": "large", "setting_def": {"piece_chars": 2400, "k": 3, "rerank": false, "router": true}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "qid": "Q1", "request_id": "chaos-20261110T183141Z-0005", "start_time": "2026-11-10T18:33:01.405+00:00", "arrival_offset_s": 79.946, "patience_s": 21.36, "gave_up": false, "waited_ms": 2908.0, "degraded": false, "reply": {"request_id": "chaos-20261110T183141Z-0005", "question": "In simple terms, what does scaled dot-product attention compute, and why divide the query-key dot products by sqrt(d_k)?", "kind": "answer", "level": 0, "text": "In simple terms, scaled dot-product attention computes a weighted sum of input values, where the weight for each value is determined by the compatibility between a query and its corresponding key [S2]. This compatibility is measured by calculating the dot product of the query vector with all key vectors [S1]. The raw dot products are then divided by the square root of the query and key dimension, denoted as $d_k$, and a softmax function is applied to these scaled scores to generate the final attention weights [S1]. Dividing by $\\sqrt{d_k}$ is necessary to prevent the dot products from becoming too large in magnitude when the dimensionality is high [S1]. If the dot products grow too large, it pushes the softmax function into regions where it has extremely small gradients, which hinders effective learning [S1]. This scaling ensures that the attention mechanism remains stable and efficient during training for larger values of $d_k$ [S1].", "sources": [{"sid": "S1", "cite": "1706.03762v7 p.4 §3.2.1", "pid": "attention:2400:0006", "paper": "attention", "block_ids": ["attention-p04-b005", "attention-p04-b006", "attention-p04-b007", "attention-p04-b008", "attention-p04-b009", "attention-p04-b010", "attention-p04-b011", "attention-p04-b012", "attention-p04-b013"]}, {"sid": "S2", "cite": "1706.03762v7 pp.3-4 §3.2"